# Cursor rules for high-precision-glioma-segmentation (HPGS)

## Project context
- Academic neuroimaging pipeline for glioma segmentation and anatomical localisation.
- Manuscript: Journal of Medical Engineering & Technology (2026).
- Published PDF: `article/Lundervold_etal_High_Precision_Segmentation_of_Glioma_JMET_2026.pdf`.

## Language, style, and tooling
- Python 3.11 only.
- Formatter: ruff (line-length 100, double quotes).
- Lint: ruff (`select = E,F,W,I,B,UP,NPY,PL,RUF`). Fix on save.
- Type hints everywhere in `src/hpgs`. Use `from __future__ import annotations`.
- Docstrings: NumPy style.
- Tests: pytest; keep under `tests/`; small synthetic arrays only (no real MRI).

## Neuroimaging conventions
- Image I/O: prefer `nibabel` for NIfTI, `SimpleITK` for filters, `ants` (ANTsPyX) for
  registration. Avoid writing our own I/O.
- Keep everything in **native subject space** where possible (avoid template warping
  unless explicitly required). SynthSeg and SynthSR give parcellations / synthesised
  contrasts in native space.
- Orientation: respect the input affine; do not silently resample.
- Units: voxel coordinates are `(i, j, k)` tuples; world coordinates are `(x, y, z)`
  mm. Never mix.
- Tumor label convention (BraTS-style, confirm per dataset):
  `0 = background, 1 = NCR/necrotic, 2 = edema, 4 = enhancing`.
  Whole tumor (WT) = {1, 2, 4}, Tumor core (TC) = {1, 4}, Enhancing tumor (ET) = {4}.

## Hardware
- Primary: Apple Silicon (M4 Max). Use `torch.backends.mps.is_available()` gating and
  fall back to CPU for ops not supported on MPS.
- Secondary: CUDA on HPC for full-cohort runs.
- Never hard-code device; read from config.

## Dependencies to prefer
- `monai` + `fastmonai` for segmentation model plumbing.
- `nnunetv2` for pre-trained BraTS-style tumor segmentation.
- `antspyx` for registration.
- FreeSurfer 8.2.0 is called as a subprocess; wrap commands in `hpgs.parcellate`.

## Data-handling rules
- **Never commit** NIfTI, DICOM, or any real patient data. `.gitignore` already blocks
  `*.nii.gz`, `data/`, etc.
- Do not upload to external services without explicit user confirmation.
- Intermediate outputs go under `data/derivatives/sub-XXXX/...`.

## Paper subfolder (`paper/`)
- The author manuscript is `paper/main.tex`, with `paper/references.bib`. The published PDF is in `article/`.
- Figures referenced by the paper live in `paper/figs/` and are mirrored from
  `outputs/figures/` by `scripts/sync_figs_to_paper.py`. Never hand-edit a figure in
  `paper/figs/` — fix the generating notebook/script under `notebooks/` or
  `src/hpgs/viz`.
- When adding a figure to the manuscript: (1) generate via code, (2) `make sync`,
  (3) `\includegraphics{figs/<name>}`, (4) update `paper/figs/README.md` inventory.
- New references go in `paper/references.bib`.
- Do not commit TeX build artefacts (`.aux`, `.log`, `.bbl`, etc.).
  `paper/main.pdf` is the tracked author-manuscript PDF. The published article
  PDF in `article/` is the version of record. `make paper` rebuilds `paper/main.pdf`.

## LaTeX authoring conventions
- **Local editor of record:** TeXShop (MacTeX) on macOS. Open `paper/main.tex`
  and typeset with `pdflatex` + `bibtex`, or run `make paper` (`latexmk`).
  Do not introduce features that require a different engine (no LuaLaTeX-only
  packages, no biber-only `.bib` syntax) unless discussed first.
- `paper/main.tex` is the author manuscript: ordinary text, the figures in
  `paper/figs/`, and `paper/references.bib`. It compiles to a 49-page A4 PDF.
  That PDF is not the journal house style. The version of record is
  `article/Lundervold_etal_High_Precision_Segmentation_of_Glioma_JMET_2026.pdf`.

## When generating code
- Prefer small, composable functions with single responsibility.
- Log with `logging` (module-level loggers); avoid prints in library code.
- Config-driven: pull hyperparameters and paths from the Hydra/OmegaConf config
  (`configs/default.yaml`). Never hard-code paths.
- For reproducibility, honour the seed (`20260415`) in all stochastic code paths.

## Commit / PR discipline
- Conventional Commits style (`feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `test:`, `chore:`).
- One topic per PR.
- Update `CHANGELOG.md` and bump version before tagging.
